      "seconds": 77.458,
      "answer": "**Lesson Summary for an 8-year-old**\n\n**Evaporation**\nImagine you have a cup of hot chocolate on a cold day. As the hot chocolate warms up, it turns into a gas and rises up into the air. This is like evaporation. The sun warms the water in lakes, and some of it turns into water vapor and rises into the air.\n\n**Condensation**\nNow, imagine you breathe onto a cold window. The warm air from your breath makes the window fog up. This is like condensation. When the water vapor in the air gets cold, it turns back into tiny liquid drops, forming clouds.\n\n**Precipitation**\nThink of a big bucket of water that's been filled up with water vapor. When the bucket gets too heavy, the water falls out as rain. This is like precipitation. When the drops become heavy, they fall as rain, and the water returns to streams and lakes.\n\n> Coverage check: 3 of 3 source sections have no linked substantive passage in this answer. The response may be incomplete; full coverage is not verified.",
